[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Angular Built-in Directives: ngIf, ngFor, and ngSwitch"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Angular directives are instructions in the DOM that tell Angular's template compiler to attach a specific behavior to that DOM element or even transform the DOM element and its children. Angular comes with a set of built-in directives that offer common functionalities. This tutorial focuses on three essential structural directives: ngIf, ngFor, and ngSwitch."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"ngIf: Conditionally Displaying Content"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'ngIf' directive adds or removes an element from the DOM based on a condition. If the expression assigned to 'ngIf' evaluates to true, Angular renders the element. If it's false, Angular removes the element from the DOM."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"html
<div *ngIf="condition">
  This content is displayed only if 'condition' is true.
</div>
"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the component's TypeScript file, define the 'condition' property:"},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"typescript
import { Component } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-example',
  templateUrl: './example.component.html',
  styleUrls: ['./example.component.css']
})
export class ExampleComponent {
  condition: boolean = true; // Or false
}
"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"ngFor: Iterating Over Collections"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'ngFor' directive repeats a section of HTML for each item in a collection. It's commonly used to display lists of data."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"html
<ul>
  <li *ngFor="let item of items; let i = index"> 
    {{ i + 1 }}: {{ item.name }} - {{ item.value }}
  </li>
</ul>
"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here, 'items' is an array in the component, 'item' represents each element in the array during iteration, and 'i' is the index of the current item.  The index starts at 0."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"typescript
import { Component } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-example',
  templateUrl: './example.component.html',
  styleUrls: ['./example.component.css']
})
export class ExampleComponent {
  items: any[] = [
    { name: 'Item 1', value: 10 },
    { name: 'Item 2', value: 20 },
    { name: 'Item 3', value: 30 }
  ];
}
"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"ngSwitch: Conditional Content Switching"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'ngSwitch' directive allows you to display different content based on a switch expression. It works in conjunction with 'ngSwitchCase' and 'ngSwitchDefault'."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"html
<div [ngSwitch]="switchValue">
  <div *ngSwitchCase="'case1'">Content for case 1</div>
  <div *ngSwitchCase="'case2'">Content for case 2</div>
  <div *ngSwitchDefault>Default content</div>
</div>
"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'ngSwitch' directive is bound to the 'switchValue' property. The 'ngSwitchCase' directives specify the values to match against 'switchValue'. If none of the cases match, the content within the 'ngSwitchDefault' directive is displayed."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"typescript
import { Component } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-example',
  templateUrl: './example.component.html',
  styleUrls: ['./example.component.css']
})
export class ExampleComponent {
  switchValue: string = 'case2'; // Can be 'case1', 'case2', or any other value
}
"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices"},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use 'ngIf' sparingly for performance reasons, especially when dealing with complex components. Consider using CSS to hide/show elements instead if the element is already rendered."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"When using 'ngFor', trackBy function can significantly improve performance when the underlying data changes.  Use 'trackBy' to provide a unique identifier for each item."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"For complex conditional logic, consider creating separate components instead of relying heavily on 'ngSwitch' to improve readability and maintainability."},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"These built-in directives are fundamental to Angular development, enabling dynamic and interactive user interfaces. Understanding and utilizing them effectively is crucial for building robust and efficient Angular applications."}]
